"MAXIMAL FLOW THROUGH A NETWORK (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/5D6E55D3B06C4F7B1043BC1D82D40764/S0008414X00036890a.pdf/div-class-title-maximal-flow-through-a-network-div.pdf)\nciteturn1384view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4.6 years ago; Content type: application/pdf; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1383search13\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 243\nL0: MAXIMAL FLOW THROUGH A NETWORK\nL1: L. R. FORD, JR. AND D. R. FULKERSON\nL2: Introduction. The problem discussed in this paper was formulated by\nL3: T. Harris as follows:\nL4: \"Consider a rail network connecting two cities by way of a number of\nL5: intermediate cities, where each link of the network has a number assigned to\nL6: it representing its capacity. Assuming a steady state condition, find a maximal\nL7: flow from one given city to the other.\"\nL8: While this can be set up as a linear programming problem with as many\nL9: equations as there are cities in the network, and hence can be solved by the\nL10: simplex method (1), it turns out that in the cases of most practical interest,\nL11: where the network is planar in a certain restricted sense, a much simpler and\nL12: more efficient hand computing procedure can be described.\nL13: In §1 we prove the minimal cut theorem, which establishes that an obvious\nL14: upper bound for flows over an arbitrary network can always be achieved.\nL15: The proof is non-constructive. However, by specializing the network (§2),\nL16: we obtain as a consequence of the minimal cut theorem an effective compu-\nL17: tational scheme. Finally, we observe in §3 the duality between the capacity\nL18: problem and that of finding the shortest path, via a network, between two\nL19: given points.\nL20: 1. The minimal cut theorem.\nL21: A graph G is a finite, 1-dimensional\nL22: complex, composed of vertices a, b, c, . . . , e, and arcs a(ab), P(ac), . . . , 8(ce).\nL23: An arc a(ab) joins its end vertices a, b; it passes through no other vertices of G\nL24: and intersects other arcs only in vertices. A chain is a set of distinct arcs of\nL25: G which can be arranged as a(ab), ft (be), y(cd), . . . , ô(gh), where the vertices\nL26: a, by c, . . . , h are distinct, i.e., a chain does not intersect itself; a chain joins\nL27: its end vertices a and h.\nL28: We distinguish two vertices of G: a, the source, and b, the sink.1 A chain flow\nL29: from a to b is a couple (C; k) composed of a chain C joining a and b, and a\nL30: non-negative number k representing the flow along C from source to sink.\nL31: Each arc in G has associated with it a positive number called its capacity.\nL32: We call the graph G, together with the capacities of its individual arcs, a\nL33: network. A flow in a network is a collection of chain flows which has the pro-\nL34: perty that the sum of the numbers of all chain flows that contain any arc is\nL35: no greater than the capacity of that arc. If equality holds, we say the arc is\nL36: saturated by the flow. A chain is saturated with respect to a flow if it contains\nL37: Received September 20, 1955.\nL38: xThe case in which there are many sources and sinks with shipment permitted from any\nL39: source to any sink is obviously reducible to this.\nL40: 399\nL41: https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5 Published online by Cambridge University Press\nL42: 400\nL43: L. R. FORD, JR. AND D. R. FULKERSON\nL44: a saturated arc. The value of a flow is the sum of the numbers of all the chain\nL45: flows which compose it.\nL46: It is clear that the above definition of flow is not broad enough to include\nL47: everything that one intuitively wishes to think of as a flow, for example,\nL48: sending trains out a dead end and back or around a circuit, but as far as\nL49: effective transportation is concerned, the definition given suffices.\nL50: A disconnecting\nL51: set is a collection of arcs which has the property that every\nL52: chain joining a and b meets the collection. A disconnecting set, no proper\nL53: subset of which is disconnecting, is a cut. The value of a disconnecting set D\nL54: (written v(D)) is the sum of the capacities of its individual members. Thus\nL55: a disconnecting set of minimal value is automatically a cut.\nL56: THEOREM 1.\nL57: (Minimal cut theorem). The maximal\nL58: flow\nL59: value\nL60: obtainable\nL61: in a network N is the minimum\nL62: of v(D) taken over all disconnecting\nL63: sets D.\nL64: Proof.\nL65: There are only finitely many chains joining a and b, say n of them.\nL66: If we associate with each one a coordinate in w-space, then a flow can be\nL67: represented by a point whose 7th coordinate is the number attached to the\nL68: chain flow along the j t h chain. With this representation, the class of all flows\nL69: is a closed, convex poly tope in w-space, and the value of a flow is a linear\nL70: functional on this polytope. Hence, there is a maximal flow, and the set of all\nL71: maximal flows is convex.\nL72: Now let S be the class of all arcs which are saturated in every maximal flow.\nL73: LEMMA 1.\nL74: S is a disconnecting\nL75: set.\nL76: Suppose not. Then there exists a chain «i, a2j . . . , am joining a and b with\nL77: at $S for each i. Hence, corresponding to each au there is a maximal\nL78: flow/*\nL79: in which at is unsaturated. But the average of these flows,\nL80: is maximal and at is unsaturated by / for each i. Thus the value of/ may be\nL81: increased by imposing a larger chain flow on «i, a2, • . . , oim, contradicting\nL82: maximality.\nL83: Notice that the orientation assigned to an arc of 5 by a positive chain flow\nL84: of a maximal flow is the same for all such chain flows. For suppose first that\nL85: (Ci, ki), (C2, k2) are two chain flows occurring in a maximal flow/, ki > k2 > 0,\nL86: where\nL87: C\\ = ai(a &i), a2(aia2),\nL88: . . . , ajidj-i,\nL89: dj), . . . , a r(a r_i, b)\nL90: C2 = Pi(a 60, /32(&i&2), • • • , /3*(&*-i, h),...,\nL91: (3s(bs_u b),\nL92: and ajiaj-i,\nL93: a3) = pk(bk-U\