 of a maximal flow through a network N as the\nL152: capacity of N (cap (N)). Then note the following corollary of the minimal cut\nL153: theorem.\nL154: COROLLARY. Let A be a collection of arcs of a network N which meets each cut\nL155: of N in just one arc. If Nr is a network obtained from N by adding k to the capacity\nL156: of each arc of A, then cap (Nf) = cap (N) + k.\nL157: It is worth pointing out that the minimal cut theorem is not true for net-\nL158: works with several sources and corresponding sinks, where shipment is re-\nL159: stricted to be from a source to its sink. For example, in the network (Fig. 1)\nL160: with shipment from a* to £* and capacities as indicated, the value of a minimal\nL161: disconnecting set (i.e., a set of arcs meeting all chains joining sources and\nL162: corresponding sinks) is 4, but the value of a maximal flow is 3.\nL163: 2\nL164: 2 X\nL165: \\ 2\nL166: a, ,o 3\nL167: \" b, ,a2\nL168: Fig. 1\nL169: 2. A computing procedure for source-sink planar networks.2 We say\nL170: that a network N is planar with respect to its source and sink, or briefly,\nL171: N is afr-planar, provided the graph G of N, together with arc ab, is a planar\nL172: 2It was conjectured by G. Dantzig, before a proof of the minimal cut theorem was obtained,\nL173: that the computing procedure described in this section would lead to a maximal flow for planar\nL174: networks.\nL175: https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5 Published online by Cambridge University Press\nL176: FLOW THROUGH A NETWORK\nL177: 403\nL178: graph (2; 3). (For convenience, we suppose there is no arc in G joining a and\nL179: b.) The importance of a&-planar networks lies in the following theorem.\nL180: THEOREM 2. If N is ab-planar, there exists a chain joining a and b which\nL181: meets each cut of N precisely once.\nL182: Proof. We may assume, without loss of generality, that the arc db is part\nL183: of the boundary of the outside region, and that G lies in a vertical strip with a\nL184: located on the left bounding line of the strip, b on the right. Let T be the\nL185: chain joining a and b which is top-most in N. T has the desired property,\nL186: as we now show. Suppose not. Then there is a cut D, at least two arcs of which\nL187: are in T. Let these be a\\ and a2l with a\\ occurring before a2 in following T\nL188: from a to b. Since D is a cut, there is a chain G joining a and b which meets\nL189: D in a\\ only. Similarly there is a chain C2 meeting D in a2 only. Let C2 be\nL190: that part of G joining a to an end point of a2. It follows from the definition\nL191: of T that G and C2 must intersect. But now, starting at a, follow C2 to its\nL192: last intersection with G, then G to b. We thus have a chain from a to b not\nL193: meeting D, contradicting the fact that D is a cut.\nL194: Symmetrically, of course, the bottom-most chain of N has the same\nL195: property.\nL196: Notice that this theorem is not valid for networks which are not a^-planar.\nL197: A simple example showing this is provided by the \"gas, water, electricity\"\nL198: graph (Fig. 2), in which every chain joining a and b meets some cut in three\nL199: arcs.\nL200: Fig. 2\nL201: Theorem 2 and the corollary to Theorem 1 provide an easy computational\nL202: procedure for determining a maximal flow in a network of the kind here\nL203: considered. Simply locate a chain having the property of Theorem 2; this can\nL204: be done at a glance by finding the two regions separated by arc db, and taking\nL205: the rest of the boundary of either region (throwing out portions of the boundary\nL206: where it has looped back and intersected itself, so as to get a chain). Impose\nL207: as large a chain flow (T\\k) as possible on this chain, thereby saturating one\nL208: or more of its arcs. By the corollary, subtracting k from each capacity in T\nL209: reduces the capacity of N by k. Delete the saturated arcs, and proceed as\nL210: https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5 Published online by Cambridge University Press\nL211: 404\nL212: L. R. FORD, JR. AND D. R. FULKERSON\nL213: before. Eventually, the graph disconnects, and a maximal flow has been\nL214: constructed.\nL215: 3. A minimal path problem. For source-sink planar networks, there is an\nL216: interesting duality between the problem of finding a chain of minimal capacity-\nL217: sum joining source and sink and the network capacity problem, which lies\nL218: in the fact that chains of N joining source and sink correspond to cuts (relative\nL219: to two particular vertices) of the dual3 of N and vice versa. More precisely,\nL220: suppose one has a network N, planar relative to two vertices a and b, and\nL221: wishes to find a chain joining a and b such that the sum of the numbers assigned\nL222: to the arcs of the chain is minimal. An easy way to solve this problem is as\nL223: follows. Add the arc ab, and construct the dual of the resulting graph G.\nL224: Let a' and V be the vertices of the dual which lie in the regions of G separated\nL225: by ab. Assign each number of the original network to the corresponding\nL226: arc in the dual. Then solve the capacity problem relative to a' and V for the\nL227: dual network by the procedure of §2. A minimal cut thus constructed corres-\nL228: ponds to a minimal chain in the original network.\nL229: 3The dual of a planar graph G is formed by taking a vertex inside each region of G and\nL230: connecting vertices which lie in adjacent regions by arcs. See (2; 3).\nL231: REFERENCES\nL232: 1. G. B. Dantzig, Maximization\nL233: of a linear function\nL234: of variables subject to linear\nL235: inequalities:\nL236: Activity analysis of production and allocation (Cowles Commission, 1951).\nL237: 2. H. Whitney, Non-separable and planar graphs, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc, 34 (1932), 339-362.\nL238: 3.\nL239: , Planar graphs, Fundamenta Mathematicae, 21 (1933), 73-84.\nL240: Rand Corporation,\nL241: Santi Monica, California\nL242: https://doi.org/10.4153/CJM-1956-045-5 Published online by Cambridge University Press\n"
